                Text {
                    text: "Found it — the recorder isn't broken, but it records **one file per process**, not per session, and Zed keeps the agent-server process alive across sessions.\n\n**What the data shows:** There's exactly one file, `2026-08-03T20-08-59Z.jsonl`, created at 16:08 local and still growing at 16:23. Inside it there are 2 `session/new` calls, 1 `session/load`, and **3 distinct sessionIds** — all of your \"new sessions\" from the last 15 minutes are in that one file.\n\n**Why:** In `main()` the recording path is computed once from `Utc::now()` at process startup, and there's one writer task for the lifetime of the process. Zed spawns your agent-server binary once and then multiplexes: each new session in the Zed UI is just a `session/new` (or `session/load`) JSON-RPC call over the same stdio connection to the same process. The recorder never sees a \"new session\" as an event worth rotating on — it just taps the channel and appends everything to the file named at spawn time. You'll only get a new file when Zed restarts the agent process (e.g. Zed restart, or the agent server gets killed).\n\n**Your other two theories:**\n- **Buffering** — not the issue. `run_writer` flushes after every line, and the mtime confirms lines are landing in real time.\n- **Timestamp overwriting** — not what's happening here, but it *is* a latent bug: `File::create` truncates, and the name only has second precision, so two processes spawned within the same second would clobber each other. Using `OpenOptions::create_new` (or appending) would make that fail loudly instead.\n\nIf you want one JSONL per session, the fix is to key recordings off the ACP session rather than the process: watch the tapped messages in `record_channel` for `session/new` / `session/load` responses, and route each line to a per-sessionId file (named e.g. `<timestamp>-<sessionId>.jsonl`). Pre-session traffic (`initialize`) could go to the first session's file or a shared prelude. Want me to make that change?",
                },
            ],
