                Text {
                    text: "Done — new recorder installed, tests passing, and your existing recording is backfilled into per-session files.\n\n**Recorder changes** (`crates/agent_session/examples/agent_session_recorder.rs`): recordings are now one file per ACP session, named `~/.agent_runtime_sessions/<session-id>.jsonl`. Since one process multiplexes many sessions, a `SessionRouter` attributes each recorded line: lines carrying `params.sessionId` route directly; responses (which never name a session) are matched to their request by JSON-RPC id, scoped by direction since the two peers' id spaces are independent; a `session/new` request is held until its response mints the id, then both lines open that session's file. Connection-level traffic (`acp_ready`, the `initialize` handshake) is copied into every session file so each recording replays standalone, and files open in append mode so a session resumed by a later process continues its existing file. Also, still flushed per line — buffering was never the issue. 7 tests pass (router attribution, direction-scoped ids, drain on disconnect, and an end-to-end writer test), fmt and clippy are clean.\n\n**Backfill**: your single `2026-08-03T20-08-59Z.jsonl` split into three session files (139, 14, and 238 lines). I verified every line parses, each file references only its own sessionId, and every source line landed in a file — except 10 lines timestamped seconds after the split ran, which is this very conversation: **you're talking to me through the old recorder process right now**, and it's still appending to the timestamp file.\n\nSo one manual step remains, in this order:\n\n1. When you're done with this session, restart the agent server in Zed (the new binary is already installed at `~/.cargo/bin/agent_session_recorder`).\n2. Re-run `python3 ~/.agent_runtime_sessions/split_recording.py ~/.agent_runtime_sessions/2026-08-03T20-08-59Z.jsonl` to pick up the tail — it rewrites the session files from scratch, so run it **before** starting new recorded sessions (a resumed session would otherwise get clobbered).\n3. Delete the old timestamp file once you're happy.\n\nThe changes are uncommitted in your working copy; let me know if you want a jj commit/PR for them.",
